 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Why do houses up north need foundations four feet deep?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Water in the soil freezes in winter, and ice takes up about nine percent more space than water.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So the frozen ground swells, and lifts anything sitting in it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In spring it thaws and drops back down. But never evenly.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Up and down, every year, until the walls crack and the doors stop closing.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But frost only reaches so deep. Below that line, the ground never freezes.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So builders set the footings below the frost line. In Minnesota, that's up to five feet.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The ice can lift the dirt around them, but not the house standing on them.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Down south, a few inches is enough. Now you know.",
   "pausa": 0.2
  }
 ],
